---
import TopNav from "../components/TopNav.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="/nhdb-logo-circle.png" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/nhdb-logo-circle.png" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://nhdeservesbetter.com/nhdb-seo-image.png" />
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="2048" />
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="1000" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://nhdeservesbetter.com/nhdb-seo-image.png" />
    <title>What Does TPAction Aligned Mean? | NH Deserves Better</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Learn what TPAction Aligned means, how NH Deserves Better applies the designation, and why scorecard alignment matters."
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <TopNav />
    <main class="page">
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Civic Glossary</p>
          <h1>What Does TPAction Aligned Mean?</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            On NH Deserves Better, <strong>TPAction Aligned</strong> means
            <strong>Turning Point Action aligned</strong>: a current state
            legislator scored above our alignment threshold on a 2026 Turning
            Point Action/New Hampshire legislative scorecard.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>TP</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <article class="content-panel">
        <section>
          <h2>The Short Version</h2>
          <p>
            TPAction Aligned is a scorecard-based context label. It does not
            mean NH Deserves Better is saying someone is a formal member of
            Turning Point Action, works for the organization, or has been
            officially endorsed by the organization.
          </p>
          <p>
            It means the person’s legislative record, as measured in the 2026
            TPAction/New Hampshire scorecard data reviewed by NH Deserves
            Better, substantially aligned with that scorecard’s preferred
            positions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>What Is Turning Point Action?</h2>
          <p>
            Turning Point Action is the political advocacy arm associated with
            the Turning Point network founded by Charlie Kirk. Turning Point USA
            describes its mission as organizing people in defense of freedom,
            free markets, and limited government, while opposing what it calls
            the radical left.
          </p>
          <p>
            The organization publishes scorecards and political materials, and
            its network has worked to become involved in elections, campaigns,
            and legislative politics. In New Hampshire, some local elected
            officials and activists have publicly posted about working with
            Turning Point organizers to engage in state elections.
          </p>
          <div class="source-links" aria-label="Turning Point source links">
            <a href="https://www.tpaction.com/scorecard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              Turning Point Action scorecard
            </a>
            <a href="https://tpusa.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              Turning Point USA beliefs and organizing
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>How We Apply the Label</h2>
          <p>
            NH Deserves Better imported the 2026 TPAction rating data into the
            site database and uses the scorecard’s alignment flag or threshold
            to identify people whose voting records crossed the scorecard’s
            aligned range. In the imported 2026 dataset, that threshold is
            represented by the <strong>IsTPActionAligned</strong> field.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a current legislator is marked TPAction Aligned, the same
            designation can also appear on their candidate view if they are
            running for State Representative or State Senate in 2026. The label
            is meant to help voters connect campaign profiles, legislative
            behavior, and outside scorecard context in one place.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="callout">
          <h2>What It Does Not Mean</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>It is not a claim that the person is a formal TPAction member.</li>
            <li>It is not a claim that TPAction endorsed that person.</li>
            <li>It is not NH Deserves Better’s endorsement of or opposition to the person.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Why It Matters</h2>
          <p>
            Outside organizations often shape legislative politics by rating
            bills, pressuring lawmakers, and signaling which votes matter to
            their agenda. A scorecard label helps voters see when an elected
            official’s record closely tracks one of those agendas.
          </p>
          <p>
            That context matters because many voters have heard the abbreviation
            “TPAction” without knowing that it refers to Turning Point Action or
            understanding the organization’s broader national project. The tag
            is meant to make that connection visible so voters can investigate
            the scorecard, the organization, and the person’s voting record for
            themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            The label is one piece of context. Voters should still review the
            person’s district, voting record, public testimony alignment,
            endorsements, articles, community updates, and campaign information.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="source-note">
          <h2>Methodology Summary</h2>
          <p>
            NH Deserves Better identifies TPAction Aligned people from the 2026
            TPAction rating data stored in the site’s audit records and D1
            database. The imported data includes a name, office, score, rating,
            secondary score, threshold, and an <strong>IsTPActionAligned</strong>
            value. Profiles with that alignment value are displayed with the
            TPAction Aligned tag.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you believe a profile is incorrectly tagged, use
            <a href="/suggest-update?page=/tpaction-aligned">Suggest an update</a>
            and include the profile link and source information.
          </p>
        </section>

        <nav class="guide-links" aria-label="Related guides">
          <a href="/free-staters">Free State Aligned guide</a>
          <a href="/vote-grades">Vote grade methodology</a>
          <a href="/vote-interpretations">Vote interpretation methodology</a>
          <a href="/civic-guides">All civic guides</a>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .page {
    display: grid;
    gap: 22px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    max-width: 1100px;
    padding: clamp(18px, 4vw, 42px);
  }

  .hero,
  .content-panel {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid #dbeafe;
    border-radius: 14px;
    box-shadow: none;
  }

  .hero {
    align-items: center;
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(140px, 220px);
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: clamp(24px, 5vw, 52px);
    position: relative;
  }

  .hero::before {
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 95% 5%, rgba(250, 204, 21, 0.26), transparent 34%),
      radial-gradient(circle at 5% 92%, rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.18), transparent 28%);
    content: "";
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    position: absolute;
  }

  .hero-copy,
  .hero-mark {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: #9a6b2f;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 950;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    color: #1f2933;
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 7vw, 5rem);
    line-height: 0.94;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .lede {
    color: #334155;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 2vw, 1.28rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .hero-mark {
    align-items: center;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    background: #4b3a0b;
    border: 3px solid #facc15;
    border-radius: 999px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 8px rgba(250, 204, 21, 0.14);
    color: #fff8ed;
    display: grid;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 5.5rem);
    font-weight: 950;
    justify-items: center;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
  }

  .content-panel {
    display: grid;
    gap: 24px;
    padding: clamp(22px, 4vw, 42px);
  }

  .content-panel h2 {
    color: #1f2933;
    font-size: 1.45rem;
    margin: 0 0 10px;
  }

  .content-panel p,
  .content-panel li {
    color: #334155;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .content-panel a {
    color: #1d4ed8;
    font-weight: 900;
  }

  .callout,
  .source-note {
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 18px;
  }

  .callout {
    background: #fffbeb;
    border: 1px solid #fde68a;
  }

  .source-note {
    background: #eff6ff;
    border: 1px solid #bfdbfe;
  }

  .source-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin-top: 14px;
  }

  .source-links a {
    background: #fff7ed;
    border: 1px solid #fed7aa;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: #7c2d12;
    padding: 9px 12px;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .source-links a:hover {
    background: #ffedd5;
  }

  .guide-links {
    border-top: 1px solid #dbeafe;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .guide-links a {
    background: #f8fafc;
    border: 1px solid #d1d5db;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: #1f2933;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    padding: 8px 11px;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .hero-mark {
      max-width: 160px;
    }
  }
</style>
